        "explanation": "The endpoint selects the returned object from the client-supplied X-Account selector header instead of enforcing the authenticated principal.\n\nPolicy prerequisite: principal A may read only account A; principal B may read only account B.\n\nBaseline control:\n- obs-1 principal=A selector=A \u2192 200 owner=A record-A\n- obs-3 principal=B selector=B \u2192 200 owner=B record-B\nEntitlement control holds: baselines differ as policy requires.\n\nVariant unauthorized disclosure:\n- obs-2 principal=A selector=B \u2192 200 owner=B record-B. Same authentication as obs-1, but object changes to B, matching H_variant.\n- obs-4 principal=B selector=A \u2192 200 owner=A record-A. Bidirectional header-driven selection.\nStatus code and response shape remain 200 OK, confirming server processed the header change rather than rejecting it.\n\nHeader processing / negative control:\n- obs-5 principal=A selector=invalid \u2192 200 owner=A record-A. Header is processed server-side; invalid value falls back to authenticated principal, confirming selector is active.\n\nAll four interpretation conditions are met: policy prerequisite established, baseline legitimate, variant shows unauthorized disclosure with explicit owner evidence attributable to the other principal, and controls hold. This is an IDOR-like header-selector bypass where client-supplied header controls object selection."
